TryHackMe: SOC L1 Alert Reporting
Platform: TryHackMe
Room: SOC L1 Alert Reporting
Difficulty: Medium
Date: 2026
Objective
Understand how to analyze and triage SOC Level 1 security alerts, identify suspicious activity patterns, and determine whether behavior indicates a potential compromise or requires escalation.
Step 1: Alert Context Understanding
The alert indicated a spike in Active Directory reconnaissance commands executed on a Windows Server host. These commands are typically used by attackers after initial compromise to map the domain environment and identify privileged accounts.
Key indicators included:
Execution of AD enumeration commands (net group, nltest, whoami /priv)
Suspicious process lineage involving IIS (w3wp.exe)
Reverse shell execution from a public user directory
SYSTEM-level execution context
Step 2: Command Analysis
Observed commands:
dir
hostname
whoami /priv
net group "Domain Admins" /domain
nltest /dclist:tryhackme.thm
These commands are commonly used for:
Privilege enumeration
Domain structure discovery
Identifying domain controllers and admin groups
This behavior strongly aligns with post-exploitation reconnaissance.
Step 3: Process Tree Investigation
The execution chain revealed a clear compromise pattern:
Grandparent Process: C:\Windows\System32\inetsrv\w3wp.exe (IIS worker process)
Parent Process: C:\Users\Public\revshell.exe (reverse shell)
Source Process: C:\Windows\System32\cmd.exe
This indicates likely web application exploitation leading to remote code execution and shell access.
Step 4: Risk Evaluation
This activity is highly suspicious due to:
Unauthorized command execution under SYSTEM privileges
Presence of reverse shell payload
AD enumeration behavior typical of lateral movement preparation
Execution originating from a web server process (common attack vector)
Potential impact:
Full domain compromise risk
Credential harvesting
Lateral movement across network
Persistence establishment
Step 5: Response & Mitigation
Recommended SOC actions:
Immediately isolate affected host (DMZ-MSEXCHANGE-2013)
Investigate IIS logs for initial exploitation vector
Analyze revshell.exe for C2 communication and persistence
Perform credential resets for potentially exposed accounts
Review domain controller logs for lateral movement activity
Conduct full incident scope assessment
Step 6: Outcome
This alert demonstrates how attackers leverage web server vulnerabilities to gain initial access and then perform Active Directory reconnaissance to escalate and move laterally within a network.
Lessons Learned
Web-facing servers are common entry points for attackers
AD enumeration commands are strong indicators of post-exploitation activity
Process tree analysis is critical in identifying attack chains
SYSTEM-level execution from unexpected sources is a major red flag
Early detection of reconnaissance can prevent full domain compromise
Tools & Techniques Used
Windows command-line enumeration analysis
Process tree investigation
Active Directory reconnaissance detection patterns
SOC alert triage methodology
